    {
      "question": "e-5418",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "8caa8137c828b6ff1939ea3a782ed1d1a00a5b37a81fbd5a230f5c23c2803246",
      "new_sha256": "65f8ae41d28cbdefdcbc321e695d31c5ff8a1016ac06ed3fb978fc64da732c9b",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-5418",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "To find the rank of $A,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068273/example-5418.json",
          "file_sha256": "7275117e649879c576ac9d4c2eb83bc36f85c9bd690c7e566f5ca9e51bcce6a4"
        }
